/**
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs Green's functions
 * @brief Generic containers for Green's functions, self energies and other important functions in quantum many-body
 * physics.
 *
 * @details The @ref triqs-gfs in the **TRIQS** library are generic containers for functions which are not known
 * analytically. As the name suggests, they are intended to be used for quantum many-body Green's functions, self
 * energies or other correlation functions, but they can in principle be used for more general functions.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-mc MC tools
 * @brief Tools to implement and perform Monte Carlo simulations.
 *
 * @details The @ref triqs-mc module provides a generic framework for writing Markov-chain Monte Carlo simulations. It 
 * does not implement any particular physical algorithm; instead it supplies the scaffolding common to all such
 * simulations — driving the Markov chain, dispatching user-defined updates, accumulating user-defined observables, and 
 * aggregating results across MPI ranks.
 *
 * A simulation is described by three ingredients supplied by the user:
 * - a **sign type** (real or complex, see triqs::mc_tools::DoubleOrComplex) that encodes the per-configuration weight,
 * - a set of **moves** that propose updates of the Markov chain and report their Metropolis acceptance ratio (see 
 * @ref triqs-mc-moves and triqs::mc_tools::MCMove),
 * - a set of **measurements** that accumulate observables along the chain (see @ref triqs-mc-measures and 
 * triqs::mc_tools::MCMeasure).
 *
 * These ingredients are plugged into a triqs::mc_tools::mc_generic instance, which manages *warmup* (equilibration, 
 * optional move calibration) and *accumulation* (measurement) phases, organised into cycles of MC steps. The driver is 
 * MPI-aware: random seeds, exception propagation, cycle counters and measurement reduction are all coordinated through 
 * an `mpi::communicator`, so the same simulation code runs serially or in parallel.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-mc
 * @defgroup triqs-mc-concepts MC concepts
 * @brief Concepts that specify the requirements for MC moves and measurements.
 *
 * @details The MC framework is generic: any user type that satisfies the relevant concept can be plugged into 
 * triqs::mc_tools::mc_generic. The concepts in this group spell out the minimal interface those types must expose:
 * - triqs::mc_tools::DoubleOrComplex constrains the *sign type* to either `double` or `std::complex<double>`.
 * - triqs::mc_tools::MCMove requires `attempt()` and `accept()` returning the sign type, with optional `reject()`, 
 * `calibrate()` and `collect_statistics()` hooks for adaptive moves and diagnostics.
 * - triqs::mc_tools::MCMeasure requires `accumulate(sign)` and `collect_results(mpi::communicator)`, leaving the 
 * storage of partial accumulators entirely to the user.
 *
 * Because the framework relies on concepts rather than virtual inheritance, user moves and measurements remain plain, 
 * value-semantic types; the triqs::mc_tools::mc_generic engine wraps them through type erasure internally.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-mc
 * @defgroup triqs-mc-measures MC measurements
 * @brief Abstraction for Monte Carlo measurements.
 *
 * @details A *measurement* is any object that, given the current sign of the Markov chain, contributes to one or more 
 * running estimators. This group provides the type-erased wrappers used by triqs::mc_tools::mc_generic to store 
 * heterogeneous measurements together:
 * - triqs::mc_tools::measure wraps a user object satisfying triqs::mc_tools::MCMeasure and bookkeeps the number of 
 * measurements, optional timing and an optional `report()` callback.
 * - triqs::mc_tools::measure_set is the container of registered measurements; it forwards `accumulate` and 
 * `collect_results` to every measurement it owns.
 * - triqs::mc_tools::measure_aux holds *auxiliary* callables that are run on every cycle but do not themselves 
 * accumulate an estimator (e.g. live diagnostics or periodic state dumps).
 *
 * Final reduction across MPI ranks is delegated to the user code inside `collect_results`, which receives the 
 * communicator.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-mc
 * @defgroup triqs-mc-moves MC moves
 * @brief Abstraction for Monte Carlo moves.
 *
 * @details A *move* proposes an update of the Markov chain and returns the Metropolis acceptance ratio. The framework 
 * supplies two type-erased containers:
 * - triqs::mc_tools::move wraps a user object satisfying triqs::mc_tools::MCMove, tracks per-move proposal and 
 * acceptance counters, optional timing, and forwards the optional `reject`, `calibrate` and `collect_statistics` hooks.
 * - triqs::mc_tools::move_set combines several moves with relative weights and itself satisfies the MCMove concept: on 
 * each step it draws one of its sub-moves with probability proportional to its weight, allowing composite update 
 * schemes to be assembled hierarchically.
 *
 * Acceptance ratios and timing statistics are exposed for diagnostics and are aggregated across MPI ranks when results 
 * are collected.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-mc
 * @defgroup triqs-mc-utils MC utilities
 * @brief Various utilities used with @ref triqs-mc.
 *
 * @details This group collects auxiliary building blocks used by the MC framework, most prominently the random number 
 * generator:
 * - triqs::mc_tools::random_generator is a type-erased wrapper around a pseudo-random engine. The engine is selected at 
 * construction by name (`"mt19937"`, several `"lagged_fibonacci..."` variants, `"ranlux3"`, and the built-in Mersenne 
 * Twister); samples are produced in batches into an internal buffer for efficiency. Overloaded `operator()` returns
 * either a uniform `double` in \f$ [0,1) \f$ or a uniform integer in \f$ \{0, ..., i-1 \} \f$, and the generator state 
 * can be serialised to HDF5 for reproducible restarts.
 *
 * The list of available engine names is exposed at runtime via triqs::mc_tools::random_generator_names_list.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-mc
 * @defgroup triqs-mc-simulation MC simulation
 * @brief Run and manage MC simulations.
 *
 * @details This group contains triqs::mc_tools::mc_generic, the driver that ties together moves, measurements and the 
 * random generator. A typical lifecycle is:
 * 1. construct an `mc_generic<S>` with a chosen engine name and seed,
 * 2. register moves with `add_move`, measurements with `add_measure`, and any auxiliary callables with 
 * `add_measure_aux`,
 * 3. run `warmup` (equilibrate the chain, optionally calibrate moves), then `accumulate` (perform measurements) — or 
 * the combined `warmup_and_accumulate` / `run`,
 * 4. call `collect_results` to reduce per-rank estimators across an `mpi::communicator`.
 *
 * The driver organises work into *cycles* of MC steps separated by measurement passes, and checks for user-requested 
 * termination and for exceptions on configurable intervals so that ranks can stop in a coordinated way even with 
 * asymmetric workloads.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-operators Many-body operators
 * @brief Tools for constructing and manipulating second-quantized many-body operators.
 *
 * @details This module provides classes and functions for working with many-body operators in second quantization,
 * including creation and annihilation operators, as well as algebraic operations on operator expressions.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-hilbert Hilbert space
 * @brief Tools for representing and manipulating many-body Hilbert spaces.
 *
 * @details This module provides classes for representing Fock states and many-body Hilbert spaces,
 * including support for quantum number sectors and state indexing.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-atom-diag Atomic problem diagonalization
 * @brief Exact diagonalization of atomic (local) Hamiltonians.
 *
 * @details This module provides tools for exact diagonalization of atomic Hamiltonians,
 * computation of the atomic Green's function, and related quantities needed for quantum impurity solvers.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-det-manip Determinant manipulation
 * @brief Efficient algorithms for manipulating determinants in Monte Carlo sampling.
 *
 * @details This module provides optimized algorithms for computing and updating determinants,
 * which are essential for determinantal quantum Monte Carlo methods.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-stat Statistical tools
 * @brief Tools for statistical analysis of Monte Carlo data.
 *
 * @details This module provides histograms, accumulators, and other statistical tools
 * for analyzing and post-processing Monte Carlo simulation data.
 */
